The paramedic who lost his glove and the nurse with the scratch both feel fine. Both are back at work.

4:21

4 Minuten, 21 Sekunden

Helsinki. The flight attendant who held the towel to the inspector's forehead wakes with a fever of 38.9. Her airline's doctor signs her off with flu and tells her to rest at home.

4:29

4 Minuten, 29 Sekunden

Her two children stay home from school to look after her.

4:33

4 Minuten, 33 Sekunden

Chicago. The seatmate from 27B makes it to her granddaughter's first birthday, a day late. Twenty-two relatives crowd into a small apartment for cake.

4:39

4 Minuten, 39 Sekunden

She feels tired, blames the journey, and goes to bed early with a sore throat and a fever.

4:44

4 Minuten, 44 Sekunden

Chicago. An emergency doctor notices that two feverish, agitated [TV] Doctors at a Chicago hospital are treating two patients with an patients from different suburbs both flew in through Keflavík this week. unusual brain inflammation. Both had recently traveled through Iceland.

4:50

4 Minuten, 50 Sekunden

She calls the city health department. The airline agrees to share its passenger manifest, but its lawyers need until Monday.

4:55

4 Minuten, 55 Sekunden

Toronto. A passenger from the diverted flight spends Saturday night at a hockey game, coughing into a paper cup. He says his fever is jet lag. Nineteen thousand people are in the arena.

5:04

5 Minuten, 4 Sekunden

He leaves early, dizzy and irritated by the lights.

5:07

5 Minuten, 7 Sekunden

Helsinki. The national lab reports on the fur farm: not bird flu, [TV, Finnish] A previously unknown virus was found in the fur farm but a virus never described before, related to ones in wild carnivores. animals. The same virus was confirmed in a Finnish patient treated in Iceland.

5:13

5 Minuten, 13 Sekunden

An hour later, Reykjavík sends a sample from the inspector. It is the same virus. Iceland asks for the passenger list.

5:18

5 Minuten, 18 Sekunden

Reykjavík. Health officials pull the airport's rebooking records: 312 names, 41 onward flights, 19 cities. They split the list by country and start calling.

5:25

5 Minuten, 25 Sekunden

Many numbers are office lines, closed on Sunday; others ring in languages nobody on the team speaks.

5:30

5 Minuten, 30 Sekunden

Helsinki. Health officials call the flight attendant at home and ask whom she has seen since Wednesday.

5:34

5 Minuten, 34 Sekunden

The list runs to forty names: neighbours, a taxi driver, her children's teachers. Her children are well, for now. Their school has six hundred pupils.

5:41

5 Minuten, 41 Sekunden

Reykjavík. The nurse with the scratch and the paramedic who lost his glove both develop fevers.

5:45

5 Minuten, 45 Sekunden

Iceland asks every driver, nurse and hotel worker who came into contact with the flight, more than two thousand people, to stay home and report their temperature twice a day. Three hotels close.

5:52

5 Minuten, 52 Sekunden

Chicago. On Monday morning the manifest arrives: 111 people from the flight landed here, and the city reaches fifty by evening.

5:58

5 Minuten, 58 Sekunden

The seatmate from 27B is among them, now confused and feverish in a suburban hospital. Her granddaughter's party had twenty-two guests.

6:04

6 Minuten, 4 Sekunden

Toronto. The hockey fan's brother drives him to [TV] Toronto health officials are asking fans who attended Saturday hospital after he fails to recognise his own front door.

6:08

6 Minuten, 8 Sekunden

Ontario asks the arena for ticket records from night's game to watch for fever. The arena is sharing its ticket records. Saturday night. Most seats were bought by card. Hundreds were resold through apps that keep no names.

6:15

6 Minuten, 15 Sekunden

Seoul. The father of the groom, two days into a fever, visits a neighbourhood clinic. The doctor prescribes flu medicine and tells him to wear a mask.

6:22

6 Minuten, 22 Sekunden

He promises to, and spends the evening at home writing seating cards by hand.

6:27

6 Minuten, 27 Sekunden

Seoul. The groom's father becomes confused at a family dinner and bites his son's hand when the son tries to take his car keys.

6:32

6 Minuten, 32 Sekunden

An ambulance takes him to hospital, where he is tested for flu and tuberculosis. The wedding is in four days.

6:38

6 Minuten, 38 Sekunden

Geneva. The World Health Organization convenes an emergency committee as cases linked to the diverted flight appear in six countries. Finland publishes the virus's genome within hours.

6:45

6 Minuten, 45 Sekunden

Labs on four continents start building tests. None will be ready for at least a week. New York. Tracers reach a passenger in Queens on the third day of her fever.

6:53

6 Minuten, 53 Sekunden

She has taken the subway to her office every day since landing. The office has three hundred employees and a single shared kitchen.

7:01

7 Minuten, 1 Sekunde

Reykjavík. The inspector is still sedated in intensive care, his kidneys strained by the fever. His wife flies in from Finland and is allowed to see him only through glass.

7:08

7 Minuten, 8 Sekunden

Doctors tell her he has a chance. They do not tell her how good a chance.

7:12

7 Minuten, 12 Sekunden

Delhi. The passenger who connected through Helsinki is traced eight days after landing, already in hospital. His fever had been treated as dengue, common here in November.

